at our first one-- is about someone getting a&nbsp;
password. And there's this kind of the question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:09.180
of security and code. So the first thing that&nbsp;
jumps out to me is, is when you're doing a scan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:15.360
the password is going to be shown as the person is&nbsp;
typing it. Now, I don't know if that was exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.160
what you were after. So that's going to put down&nbsp;
is my first one is is don't show the password.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:27.660
Right? But that might not be what you were after.&nbsp;
The other thing that kind of jumped out at me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:32.280
besides that, is when you say to save the&nbsp;
password, and this is where I was doing some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:38.640
security audits myself. And they mentioned that&nbsp;
when you have critical data, you don't want it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:45.240
ending up in logs, for example. And so here, I&nbsp;
wouldn't be passing around a password that is in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:50.700
the clear. In fact, a smart way of doing this is,&nbsp;
is you run a hash on it first. Because you really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:56.040
don't care about what the person's stored as their&nbsp;
password. You care "will the hash of it actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:00.840
match?", which is good enough for password&nbsp;
security purposes. The other upside is, is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:05.940
when you pass into the database, you can avoid&nbsp;
a potential problem where someone has inserted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:11.400
things in that password that's trying to do a SQL&nbsp;
injection. And that's where someone is trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:18.120
trick the program into running a SQL query that&nbsp;
really wasn't intended. That's the three things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:25.200
I think I was talking about -- was running a&nbsp;
hash. Make sure... Oh! And also make sure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:33.660
the password has the keywords, er, the complexity&nbsp;
that it should have. And also the SQL injection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:40.362
Those are my three answers. That is my final&nbsp;answer. &nbsp;

00:02:40.362 --> 00:02:52.380
Well, you did good, Dan, I must say that.&nbsp;So, this is an example of one from OWASP top&nbsp;ten-- this is an example of cryptographical failure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:58.500
You never store passwords in plain text. Hashing&nbsp;
with salting, that's what you want to do. [Dan] Right, exactly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:07.140
And then second thing, as you said, with&nbsp;
complexity. One other thing is, it's not checking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:13.736
you know, at a minimum, the length of password.&nbsp;
The longer the password, they tend to be more&nbsp;safer.

00:03:13.736 --> 00:03:17.878
We have a bunch of videos on that topic, as&nbsp;
a matter of fact. So be sure to check it out. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

