So here we are. Now, okay, this requires just&nbsp;a teeny bit of explanation for Q2. And you see this permission here of 0700. For the

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:04.260
Unix-- people who are newer to UNIX, let me point&nbsp;
out what that means-- that's referring to the

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:11.040
[file] permissions. It's a bit pattern where each one of&nbsp;
these numbers represents the permissions for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:17.940
owner, group, and everyone. And so, for example,&nbsp;
this says that-- it's a three bit pattern --that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:24.120
they have execute, or traverse rights, read and&nbsp;
write. That's essentially what the 7 means.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:32.220
And I have to admit, when I first looked at this,&nbsp;I didn't really see a problem because it's saying essentially, this 700 is saying "I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:36.120
going to let the owner be able to read, write&nbsp;
or traverse it", which is perfectly the legit--&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:45.060
and no one else can. Another common pattern you'll&nbsp;
see is 755 saying essentially the owner can do anything they like and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:50.760
other people can read or traverse. I had to look&nbsp;
this one up, and if you haven't hit pause already,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:54.960
here's your opportunity to go do that and look&nbsp;
it up yourself. I tried to find some potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:05:01.620
flaws here. One thing it mentioned was the umask&nbsp;
capability, where you have the masking that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:06.360
applied by the operating system to a newly&nbsp;
created file. Though I don't know if that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:12.617
what you were after. The other possibility&nbsp;
is, is that there's a coding question here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.617 --> 00:05:18.240
because you're checking to see if&nbsp;
the error that came back was nil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:25.920
but it could have already existed. So that&nbsp;
was really, one was umask, prior existing...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:34.080
And the other one? I give up. Can I phone&nbsp;
a friend? [Sahdev] Well, no, you pretty much got it,&nbsp;actually.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:35.702
Really?

00:05:35.702 --> 00:05:49.740
So the one big thing&nbsp;here is every time you use any function, here Go package mkDirAll. Or using third-party packages, right, you must understand the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:57.960
function before blindly using it. How it works.&nbsp;
So mkDirAll, it's a function that will create the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:02.340
directory for you with the desired permission,&nbsp;
if it's not there. [Dan] Yeah, if it already exists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:07.620
it'll just leave it alone. It doesn't even return&nbsp;
an error. [Sahdev] Exactly. So what's missing here is "What&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:15.660
if there's a directory there with 0777?" [Dan] Yeah, right. In fact,&nbsp;when I was looking up through Stack Overflow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:20.580
there was this big argument about a potential race&nbsp;
condition for this, too. Because as you check to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:25.020
see, is that come back in an error, and then I&nbsp;
checked to see it exists-- in between, potentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:29.580
someone could have come in and created that file.&nbsp;
And so it turns out this is actually, for such a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:34.380
small problem, it's really something to look at.&nbsp;[Sahdev] It's a small but this is one of the, like I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

