00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:52.680
so I have to comment first that I'm not really&nbsp;
happy with this as a code example because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:56.760
kind of offends from a few other ways besides&nbsp;
security that I feel compelled to point out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:03.840
One-- please don't do this where you write a&nbsp;
sleep loop. That's kind of a bad idea overall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:07.800
because that means that the process is&nbsp;
going to continue to consume resources.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:12.360
It also means the process has state in&nbsp;
it, and if it is somehow terminated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:16.980
you've lost that state. A more proper way of doing&nbsp;
this is is that you should create some sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:21.120
background daemon that you register these sleep&nbsp;
notifications-- I'm sorry --these notifications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:27.120
with and hopefully that will do the result that&nbsp;
you want. I don't think that's what you're after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:34.860
though. [Sahdev] That's true! [Dan] Yeah, but I felt compelled&nbsp;
to mention it! But I think what you're after is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:40.380
is that if you're doing this sleep notification,&nbsp;
especially if it's just for a longer period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:45.900
potentially the access rights to the person&nbsp;
you're notifying has changed in between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:53.040
time. Like imagine, for example, you're getting an&nbsp;
email notification coming from one of your systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:57.960
and they need authority to be able to do that.&nbsp;
Well, if I withdraw that authority and I have this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:02.700
background process running, potentially I'm going&nbsp;
to be notifying them, giving them information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:08.220
leaking them information that they really don't&nbsp;
have a right to do. And so in this code example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:14.340
where they're in the-- coming out of this reminder&nbsp;
--they should check to make sure that that user&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:23.460
has the [authority] to do that. [Sahdev] So, you do&nbsp;
touch a couple of good points. But the one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:33.780
the main thing I'm looking here from security risk&nbsp;
areas and which is number three in the OWASP top ten: input validation&nbsp;&nbsp;

