or data validation. So what are we doing here is,&nbsp;
we're getting the input, right? Let's say you got,&nbsp;10 minutes.

00:08:41.097 --> 00:08:47.280
Now, as you said, you know, using sleep&nbsp;
here just for simplicity, right? Only in real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:52.140
life, you know, in Golang, you may be using&nbsp;
Go routines. Write a trigger running, right? A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.140 --> 00:09:00.000
daemon running, right? And that might be running&nbsp;
for as long as the app is running and sending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:05.760
reminders or some sort of... [Dan] You're saying, for&nbsp;
example, someone passes in a 1000 minutes or something like that or repetitive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:13.740
that's very... [Sahdev] That's true. Or think about it.&nbsp;
You want a notification every ten minutes, for example, or every one hour, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:23.220
You make a little bit of logic change here. So, you&nbsp;
know, one hour for 30 days, 24 hours, or every day for the whole month.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:33.120
What if somebody passed a negative value? Or&nbsp;
zero? We're not checking it! Instead of one hour or 10 minutes or whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:40.320
you decide, it's going to send it instantly.&nbsp;
Because it's zero or negative. So that will one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:46.260
it's going to be incorrect from what your&nbsp;
expectation. Second is, we're logging it, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:54.840
Depending on, like you said, the total numbers,&nbsp;
because there's no cap here either. It can really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.740 --> 00:10:02.400
use all the hardware resources, the storage. Keep&nbsp;
logging, logging and like instantly. And your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:10.020
expectation was every hour or every few minutes&nbsp;
or so. But because you didn't check the input, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:15.900
can crash your system. [Dan] That's a good&nbsp;
point. And in fact, I would argue that if you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:21.840
looking at parameter checking, it's not a bad idea&nbsp;
to even check it between routines. When you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:27.240
